WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.510
I’ll be honest with you, this is not Mars.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.790
But it’s one of the closest simulations
we’ve got:

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:09.499
the Mars Yard, at NASA’s Jet Propulsion
Laboratory in Pasadena, California.

00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:13.780
This is where NASA tests out models of its
rovers before they get sent to the Red Planet.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.890
And over there is where NASA’s engineers
can learn to drive one.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.990
We have the Mars Yard, which is made of a
few things:

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.320
some of them are rocks that we actually take
to a lab to cook

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.630
and ensure that they have the exact same compositions,

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.750
and we bring it to the Mars Yard.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.220
Other rocks are actually not the same composition

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.320
but the colour is the same,
or the texture is the same.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.350
And the same thing with the sand.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.690
We bring sands from specific areas so that
the colour and the texture matches.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.920
Out here in the Mars Yard, there’s not just
that simple model.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.490
Over in that hanger, well, it’s not really
a model of Curiosity:

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.520
It’s a full-size physical twin.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.750
It’s not got quite all the electronics and
there’s no plutonium,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.870
but it’s close enough to figure out what
to do in tough situations.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.700
All the instruments,
all the mechanisms that we have,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.030
plus additional simulations,
we put it all together

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.440
and we integrate it right here with Maggie.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.720
80-90% is probably the same thing
we have on Mars.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.220
There’s a few things that aren’t.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.210
For example, the wheels themselves.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.409
The gravity is different on Mars,
so you don’t want to have

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:13.870
the exact same thickness of material
on the ones on Earth

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.820
otherwise you’re going to
break them very easily,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.770
and it’s not going to represent
what actually happens on Mars.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.240
So the intent is: try to dry-run
everything that we can with this one,

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.079
try to simulate it,
and then go for it on Mars.

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:28.540
Driving a rover comes with a strange problem:
a time lag that,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.710
depending on where Earth and Mars
are in their orbits,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.950
is anywhere between four minutes and 24 minutes.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.180
This is not a case of just grabbing a joystick
and powering forwards:

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.180
there is a lot of planning involved.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.869
Because if you break that rover,

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:44.759
there are no spare parts and no second chances.

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:49.810
I’ve been driving rovers since 2004, so
13 years now.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.649
I’m the person that has driven
the most kilometres on Mars.

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:54.649
Only 16.

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:58.619
We can go from really low level commands,

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:04.100
say “apply 5 volts to a motor”, up to
a command of saying

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.470
“go there and avoid all the obstacles that
are in between”.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:13.720
The vehicle not only has software on board
to control the movement,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.650
but most importantly, has software to determine
if something bad is happening.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.770
Every day we use the cameras to take pictures.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.970
The images there — there they are — so
we can reconstruct the 3-D terrain.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:34.300
From those pictures, we can determine what
activities we can do at that location

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.520
and where it is safe to drive.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:42.240
On Curiosity, we have about 20 people that
are certified to drive the vehicle.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.770
We have different styles,
different comfort levels,

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.970
in terms of risk or
things that we are scared of.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.380
Some of us are particularly scared of
sand, steep slopes.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.590
It depends on what problems
you had when you were driving.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.480
Every single day that we are going to spend
with the rover on a specific site,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.030
it’s very important for us to
“test as you fly”,

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.690
and what I mean with that is:
as much as possible, do it here,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.870
because you don’t want to be surprised when
you try it on Mars!

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.540
Thank you to all the team at the Mars Yard

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.130
and at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:13.910
Pull down the description for more about them
and their missions.

